TIDELINE

Written by
A. Test Fixture


FADE IN:

EXT. HARBOUR WALL - PRE-DAWN

Wind. A single sodium lamp. The water is black and moving.

MAREN VOSK, fifties, waterproofs over a nightdress, walks the wall with a torch. She stops where the railing is bent outward.

She crouches. Puts two fingers on the paint. It comes away wet.

                    MAREN
          (into a handheld radio)
          Station, this is Vosk. I'm at the
          east wall.

                    RADIO (V.O.)
          Go ahead, Maren.

                    MAREN
          The railing's gone. Somebody went
          over.

She sweeps the torch across the water. Nothing.

CUT TO:

INT. HARBOUR STATION - OFFICE - DAWN

Fluorescent light. A wall of tide charts, annotated in three different hands.

DEREK ALGOOD, thirty, stands with his coat still on. Maren comes in behind him and does not take hers off either.

                    DEREK
          Coastguard says four hours minimum
          before they can get a boat out.

                    MAREN
          Four hours is the whole tide.

                    DEREK
          I know what four hours is.

She goes to the chart wall. Traces a line with one finger, west to east.

                    MAREN
          If they went in at three, they're
          past the bar by now.

                    DEREK
          You don't know they went in at three.

                    MAREN
          The paint was wet.

Derek looks at the floor. Then at her boots, which are also wet.

                    DEREK
          How long were you out there?

Maren does not answer. She takes a key off the board and goes.

INT/EXT. LAUNCH BOAT - HARBOUR MOUTH - MORNING

Diesel. Spray. Maren at the wheel, Derek in the bow with a boat hook, not using it.

                    DEREK (CONT'D)
          This isn't a search. You know that.

                    MAREN
          It's a search until somebody tells
          me it isn't.

The boat crests. Ahead, the bar: a white line of broken water.

                    DEREK
          Maren.

                    MAREN
          I know.

She turns the wheel anyway.

EXT. SANDBAR - MORNING

Low water. A stretch of grey sand nobody stands on twice.

Maren walks it. Derek stays at the boat.

She finds a shoe. Holds it up. Puts it down exactly where she found it.

                    MAREN
          (to herself)
          All right.

She marks the spot with a stick and photographs it against the shoreline.

INT. HARBOUR STATION - OFFICE - NIGHT

The tide charts again. One is now marked in red.

Derek is asleep in a chair. Maren is not. She has a shoebox open on the desk — photographs, a bent railing bolt, a plastic evidence bag with a shoe in it.

She writes a date on the back of a photograph and puts it in the box.

                    DEREK
              (not opening his eyes)
          You have to sleep sometime.

                    MAREN
          I sleep.

                    DEREK
          You don't.

She closes the box. Sets it on the shelf beside four identical boxes.

FADE OUT.

THE END
